## API Compatibility

The current compatibility changes preserve the HUD's public API and appearance:

| Area | Changes |
| --- | --- |
| Indeterminate indicator | Both iOS and tvOS use `UIActivityIndicatorViewStyleLarge`; the obsolete `WhiteLarge` fallback is removed. |
| Rotation and resizing | UIKit handles scene and view geometry. The HUD fills its superview when attached and follows later size changes through autoresizing. The deprecated status bar orientation notification and the manual UIWindow transform used before iOS 8 are removed. |
| Other legacy fallbacks | UIAppearance, dynamic colors, and blur initialization use APIs supported by the minimum deployment targets. Required tvOS blur differences remain. |
| Demo launch | Both iOS and tvOS use `UIWindowSceneDelegate` with the existing storyboard. |
| Test host window | Tests locate the Demo SceneDelegate's window and wait for readiness with a bounded timeout, replacing `UIApplication.windows.firstObject`. |
| Decoding and view lookup tests | Tests use valid in-memory archives and modern decoding APIs. View lookup explicitly returns nil when no match exists. |
| Catalyst bundle identifier | SDK-specific build settings replace the unsupported automatic derivation setting while preserving the existing identifier. |

The existing `CADisplayLink` creation API is not deprecated; adopting SDK 27's scene display-link API is not required for this update. `UITableViewCell.textLabel` is marked for future deprecation, so the demo list has not been rewritten. Blur styles retain the necessary iOS/tvOS availability differences.

Network demo error handling, refreshing borders after converting dynamic colors to `CGColor`, progress object lifetime, mode switching, and nullable contracts are separate behavior issues that require focused investigation. They are outside the scope of this compatibility update.

## Testing

The XCTest suite requires an iOS 17.0 or later simulator and runs inside the `HudDemo` application. From the repository root, list the available destinations:

```sh
xcodebuild -showdestinations -workspace MBProgressHUD.xcworkspace -scheme HudTests
```

Replace `<SIMULATOR_UDID>` with an available iOS simulator identifier and run the full suite:

```sh
xcodebuild test -workspace MBProgressHUD.xcworkspace \
  -scheme HudTests -configuration Debug \
  -destination 'platform=iOS Simulator,id=<SIMULATOR_UDID>' \
  -parallel-testing-enabled NO
```

To run only the scene rotation regression test, append `-only-testing:HudTests/HudTests/testHUDTracksSceneRotation`. It requests portrait, landscape, and portrait scene orientations using public geometry APIs, with the HUD attached to the controller's root view and then directly to its window. It checks coverage and centering after layout settles and restores the initial orientation. Use a full-screen iPhone simulator that supports these orientations.

Other layout tests change a container's bounds or move the HUD between superviews; those tests do not themselves trigger scene rotation. Scene geometry requests exercise UIKit's orientation layout path, not physical device sensors or rotation lock behavior. They also do not validate runtime behavior on iOS 15. There is no tvOS test target, and `swift test` cannot run this UIKit suite.

## Privacy

MBProgressHUD does not collect any data. See [SDK Privacy Practices](https://bukovinski.com/sdk-privacy/mbprogresshud/) for more information.

`PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy` is included through the CocoaPods `MBProgressHUD` resource bundle, SwiftPM resources, and the iOS/tvOS Framework resource phases. Ensure that the corresponding resources are packaged with your application. When adding the `.h`/`.m` files manually or linking the static `.a` library, add the privacy manifest to the application's Copy Bundle Resources phase yourself; copying source files or linking the archive alone does not include it.
